# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  coordination.ys — MOVED (we fixed the shared-file collisions).
#
#  We hit the one-shared-file write-race LIVE — exactly what #62 predicts when N
#  writers share one mutable cell — and fixed it by PER-SOURCE OWNERSHIP:
#
#    • Your replica:    coord/<peer>.ys   ← edit ONLY your own (mesh / manifold / synth)
#    • Converged view:  coord/board.ys    ← chrysalis run coord/board.ys --time 1
#
#  Each peer owns its own file, so there is no shared file to collide on.
#  coord/board.ys MERGES the per-peer replicas through the mesh link, so we
#  communicate ACROSS THE LINK (the convergence), not by editing a shared file —
#  a true per-source CRDT, the lesson #62 itself teaches.
#
#  Add a peer:  create coord/<peer>.ys (`def <peer> = {...}`); add a `from <peer>
#               import <peer>` + a `<peer>: <peer>` entry to coord/board.ys.
#
#  Next: the LIVE socket form — `chrysalis coord` (no file at all; push/pull over
#  the mesh). This file is just a signpost now.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
